[Music]  i've been in this situation before  i was rescued from boats two times  these people wouldn't choose to do this  it's dangerous  but they feel like they have no choice  viewing these images the latest group of  migrants intercepted in the english  channel today  is clearly a struggle for this young  asylum seeker  rami made a similar journey just eight  months ago  forced to enter the uk illegally he says  because he was fleeing for his life  and there was no other option we lost  many good family members  in syria the people who are still alive  are the ones who left syria  my house was bombed i had to leave my  wife and daughter  thankfully they are still alive but i  have not seen them for three years  the flow of migrants attempting to cross  the channel by small boats is relentless  almost 250 yesterday dozens again today  the home secretary's planned solution  will effectively mean those who cross  illegally  will join the back of the queue when it  comes to claiming asylum  for the first time whether people enter  the uk  legally or illegally will have an impact  on how their asylum claim progresses  and on their status in the uk if that  claim is successful  these changes risk making the situation  even worse  for victims of human trafficking as it  would be even  harder to access help in the uk helping  criminal gangs  escape justice rami says no asylum  seeker wants to break the law  but for many it's the choice between  life and death  people are desperate this is not going  to stop people coming here  for those crossing the channel it's  often the only way they can reach the uk  they don't have the luxury of seeking a  safe route  what she's doing is judging asylum  seekers on the way they are arriving  which is wrong in dunkirk the makeshift  camp here is still  full of those who dream of reaching uk  shores  one young kurdish man told us whatever  pretty patel  says he still plans to make the crossing  we have only two way but or other one  so what we should do we are an  immigration person  people you know see much person is here  and  also they want to go into uk  in south end neuron is one of the lucky  ones  accepted onto the vulnerable persons  resettlement scheme  the type of program the home secretary  wants other asylum seekers to use  but neurons says that type of program  simply isn't open to the majority of  asylum seekers  it's very sad very upsetting even  sometimes  i feel guilty for coming in a safe  safer ways than they are but and then i  think it's not my fault  it's the government's fault they don't  want to take these routes but they have  to  because they don't have a choice they  want they don't want to die they don't  want their children to die  they want to save their lives the home  secretary clearly believes she has  widespread public support for her  crackdown  but implementing those plans is likely  to be far from  easy with human rights groups already  talking about taking legal action to  stop them  mark white sky news

